, 767's only out-neighbours in the window are {722, 988, 1719} (step_03) while 1635's only in-neighbours are {106, 133, 302} (step_04); the sets are disjoint and there is no direct 767\u21921635 edge, so any influence chain would need three or more hops, each with a gap of at most 600 units. Both endpoints are extremely sparse (7 events each over ~38k time units, step_02), and their activity is scattered in isolated, mostly non-overlapping bursts (step_05, step_06). Refining to 800-unit buckets (step_07, step_08), 767 fires only near ~31.1\u201331.9k and ~51.1\u201351.9k, whereas 1635 receives near ~31.1\u201331.9k, ~32.7\u201333.5k and ~52.7\u201353.5k. The only near-coincidence (~31.5k) is not preceded by a usable directed chain, and the later 1635 activity trails 767's nearest event by well over the 600-unit relay limit for the number of hops required. I therefore find no time-respecting, 600-unit-bounded cascade from 767 to 1635; the apparent connection is a static-connectivity/popularity artefact. Confidence is moderate rather than high because intermediate-node event times were not exhaustively enumerated within the remaining budget."}, "raw_final_response": "```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_049448d4\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"cascade\",\n  \"entities\": [767, 1635],\n  \"window\": [20674, 59073],\n  \"confidence\": 0.55,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"support\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"767 and 1635 sit inside a large, well-connected 2-hop component (step_01), so an order-ignoring path between them exists \u2014 but that is exactly the confound to discount. Directionally, 767's only out-neighbours in the window are {722, 988, 1719} (step_03) while 1635's only in-neighbours are {106, 133, 302} (step_04); the sets are disjoint and there is no direct 767\u21921635 edge, so any influence chain would need three or more hops, each with a gap of at most 600 units. Both endpoints are extremely sparse (7 events each over ~38k time units, step_02), and their activity is scattered in isolated, mostly non-overlapping bursts (step_05, step_06). Refining to 800-unit buckets (step_07, step_08), 767 fires only near ~31.1\u201331.9k and ~51.1\u201351.9k, whereas 1635 receives near ~31.1\u201331.9k, ~32.7\u201333.5k and ~52.7\u201353.5k. The only near-coincidence (~31.5k) is not preceded by a usable directed chain, and the later 1635 activity trails 767's nearest event by well over the 600-unit relay limit for the number of hops required. I therefore find no time-respecting, 600-unit-bounded cascade from 767 to 1635; the apparent connection is a static-connectivity/popularity artefact. Confidence is moderate rather than high because intermediate-node event times were not exhaustively enumerated within the remaining budget.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 25217, "total_output_tokens": 3428, "total_tokens": 28645, "total_latency_ms": 51047, "llm_calls": 5, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}